<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Total war and attrition</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">Total war</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> describes a recurring pattern in which prolonged, large-scale conflict drains an empire's manpower, finances and social cohesion faster than they can be replenished. Even victorious states could be fatally weakened by such struggles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> collapsed under the strains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> was exhausted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> was ground down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>.</p></div>
